<h1>Check 01: Heading Hierarchy</h1>
<p><strong>Expected:</strong> H1 through H6 should have a clear visual hierarchy. Each heading should have readable font size, reasonable top and bottom spacing, and appropriate font weight. Lower-level headings should not collapse into paragraph text.</p>

<h2>Heading Level Test: H2 Section</h2>
<p>H2 headings should clearly divide major sections. They need enough top margin to separate ideas, enough bottom margin to connect with the following paragraph, and enough weight to feel important without overwhelming the article title.</p>

<h3>Heading Level Test: H3 Subsection</h3>
<p>H3 headings are used for supporting sections. They should be visibly smaller than H2 while still standing apart from body text.</p>

<h4>Heading Level Test: H4 Detail Title</h4>
<p>H4 should work for detail groups, feature notes, and smaller explanations. It should not look like plain bold paragraph text.</p>

<h5>Heading Level Test: H5 Minor Note</h5>
<p>H5 is often forgotten in blog CSS. It should remain readable, distinct, and correctly spaced.</p>

<h6>Heading Level Test: H6 Smallest Heading</h6>
<p>H6 should not become too tiny to read on mobile screens.</p>

<hr>

<h2>Check 02: Paragraph Rhythm</h2>
<p><strong>Expected:</strong> Paragraphs should have comfortable line height, readable font size, and enough vertical spacing. Long text should wrap naturally inside the article column without creating horizontal page overflow.</p>
<p>This short paragraph checks compact reading rhythm.</p>
<p>This longer paragraph checks how a real article feels when several lines wrap naturally inside the content column. Users should be able to scan the text without feeling that lines are too tight, paragraphs are glued together, or the content area is too wide. On mobile, the line length should stay comfortable and should not force horizontal page scrolling.</p>
<p>This paragraph includes <strong>bold text</strong>, <em>italic text</em>, and <code>inline code</code>. Inline code should be visually distinct without disrupting the surrounding line height.</p>

<h2>Check 03: HTML Entities and &amp;nbsp;</h2>
<p><strong>Expected:</strong> Literal entity text such as <code>&amp;nbsp;</code> should display correctly when written as code. Real non-breaking spaces should render as spaces, keep short phrases together where appropriate, and avoid causing the whole article column to overflow on mobile.</p>
<p>Visible entity labels: <code>&amp;nbsp;</code>, <code>&amp;amp;</code>, <code>&amp;lt;</code>, and <code>&amp;gt;</code>. Rendered characters: Apple &amp; iOS, 5 &lt; 10, and 10 &gt; 5.</p>
<p>Normal spaces: iPhone 17 Pro Max / A19 Pro chip / USB-C charging / Super Retina XDR / 2TB storage / battery life.</p>
<p>Non-breaking spaces: iPhone&nbsp;17&nbsp;Pro&nbsp;Max / A19&nbsp;Pro&nbsp;chip / USB-C&nbsp;charging / Super&nbsp;Retina&nbsp;XDR / 2TB&nbsp;storage / battery&nbsp;life.</p>
<p>Repeated non-breaking-space sample: iPhone&nbsp;17&nbsp;Pro&nbsp;Max / iPhone&nbsp;17&nbsp;Pro&nbsp;Max / iPhone&nbsp;17&nbsp;Pro&nbsp;Max / iPhone&nbsp;17&nbsp;Pro&nbsp;Max.</p>

<h2>Check 04: Lists</h2>
<p><strong>Expected:</strong> Unordered lists should show bullets and indentation. Ordered lists should show numbers and indentation. Wrapped lines should align under the list text, not under the bullet or number.</p>
